【45†Plan your visit】\nL116:   * 【46†360° virtual tour】\nL117:   * 【47†Venue hire】\nL118:   * 【3†Parliament Shop†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL119: ### Keep up to date\nL120: \nL121: 【48†Visit newsletter Sign up to our newsletter to receive the latest news about visiting UK Parliament.†parliament.us14.list-manage.com】\nL122: \nL123: ## Learning\nL124: \nL125: Your UK Parliament offers free, flexible support for teachers, community groups, and home educators to spark engagement and active citizenship.\nL126: \nL127: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL128: ### In this section\nL129: \nL130:   * 【49†Learning home†learning.parliament.uk】\nL131:   * 【50†About UK Parliament education†learning.parliament.uk】\nL132:   * 【51†Teacher resources and lesson plans†learning.parliament.uk】\nL133:   * 【52†CPD Training†learning.parliament.uk】\nL134:   * 【53†Outreach†learning.parliament.uk】\nL135:   * 【54†Visit†learning.parliament.uk】\nL136: ### Supporting teachers\nL137: \nL138: 【54†Inspire your students Book a school visit, classroom workshop or teacher-training session†learning.parliament.uk】 【55†Free teacher training resources Access videos, worksheets, lesson plans and games.†learning.parliament.uk】\nL139: \nL140: ### Keep up to date\nL141: \nL142:   * 【56†Sign up to our education newsletter†parliament.us16.list-manage.com】\nL143: \nL144: ## Shop\nL145: \nL146: Shop\nL147: \nL148: 【2†Close】 【2†Back】 【2†Close】\nL149: \nL150: ### In this section\nL151: \nL152:   * 【3†Shop home†www.shop.parliament.uk】\nL153: \nL154: 【57†UK Parliament】\nL155: # Leader of the House of Lords\nL156: \nL157: The Leader of the House is a member of the Cabinet and the most senior member of the government in the Lords, responsible for its business in the House. She leads a team of about 25 ministers and whips.\nL158: \nL159: 【58†Image: There is no description available for this image (ID: 172971)】\nL160: \nL161: 【58†Image: Reflection of image】\nL162: The Leader also has obligations to the House as a whole. She expresses its collective feelings on formal occasions by giving motions of thanks and congratulations. She also gives procedural advice, eg in disputes over who will speak next during questions. The Leader is available to assist and advise all members of the Lords.\nL163: \nL164: The current Leader of the House of Lords is 【59†Baroness Smith of Basildon†members.parliament.uk】. As with all past Leaders, she was appointed to the position by the prime minister.\nL165: ## Responsibilities\nL166: \nL167:   * Leading the government benches in the House of Lords\nL168:   * Conduct of government business in the Lords (jointly responsible with the Chief Whip)\nL169:   * Repeating in the Lords statements made by the prime minister to the Commons\nL170:   * Giving guidance to the House on matters of order and procedure\nL171:   * Taking part in formal ceremonies in the House, such as the State Opening of Parliament\nL172:   * Chairman, Board of Trustees for Chevening.\nL173: ## Further information\nL174: \nL175:   * About our members: 【59†Baroness Smith of Basildon†members.parliament.uk】\nL176: \nL177: Image: House of Lords chamber. Copyright House of Lords 2022 / photography by Annabel Moeller\nL178: ## Lords Enquiry Service and FAQs\nL179: \nL180: 【60†House of Lords FAQs】\nL181: \nL182: The Lords Enquiry Service provides information on the role, work and membership of the House of Lords.\nL183: \nL184:   * Telephone: 0800 223 0855 (Freephone) or 020 7219 3107\nL185:   * Email: hlinfo@parliament.uk\nL186:   * Text Relay: 18001 7219 3107\nL187: \nL188: The telephone enquiry service is open from 10am - 5pm Monday to Thursday, and 10am - 4pm on Fridays. During recess, the hours are 10am - 1pm and 2pm - 4pm Monday to Friday.\nL189: Please note that the Enquiry Service is unable to forward emails to members of the Lords on your behalf. You can find a member's contact details on their webpage, or use contactholmember@parliament.uk.\nL190: ###### Popular\nL191: \nL192:   * 【61†What's on in Parliament†whatson.parliament.uk】\nL193:   * 【62†Petitions†petition.parliament.uk】\nL194:   * 【28†Careers at Parliament】\nL195:   * 【63†Commons Media Relations】\nL196:   * 【64†Lords Press Office】\nL197:   * 【65†Contact us】\nL198: \nL199: ###### Research\nL200: \nL201:   * 【66†Commons Library†commonslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL202:   * 【67†Lords Library†lordslibrary.parliament.uk】\nL203:   * 【68†POST†post.parliament.uk】\nL204: \nL205: ###### Search Parliament\nL206: \nL207: [Input]\nL208: \nL209: [Input]\nL210: \nL211: ###### Watch Parliament Live\nL212: \nL213: 【69†Parliament Live TV†parliamentlive.tv】\nL214: ###### Follow Parliament\nL215: \nL216: ###### Legal\nL217: \nL218:   * 【70†Accessibility statement】\nL219:   * 【31†Modern slavery statement】\nL220:   * 【71†About this site】\nL221:   * 【72†Privacy notice】\nL222:   * 【73†Cookie policy】\nL223:   * 【2†Cookie settings】\nL224: \nL225: © UK Parliament 2026\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElectoral College | USAGov (https://www.usa.gov/electoral-college)\nciteturn29view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn28view0\",\"id\":19}); Total lines: 123\nL0: 【0†GTM†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【2†Image: Close】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL5:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL6:   * 【5†Government benefits】\nL7:   * 【6†Immigration and U.S. citizenship】\nL8:   * 【7†Money and credit】\nL9:   * 【8†Travel】\nL10:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL11:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL12:     * 【10†Voter registration】\nL13:     * 【11†How, when, and where to vote】\nL14:     * 【12†Decide who to vote for】\nL15:     * 【13†Find my state or local election office】\nL16:     * 【14†Congressional, state, and local elections】\nL17:     * 【15†Voting and election laws】\nL18:     * 【16†How the president is elected】\nL19:       * 【17†Overview of the presidential election process】\nL20:       * 【18†Requirements for presidential candidates】\nL21:       * current page\nL22: \nL23: Electoral College\nL24:       * 【19†National conventions】\nL25:       * 【20†Primaries and caucuses】\nL26:       * 【21†Presidential general election】\nL27:     * 【22†Inauguration of the president】\nL28:     * 【23†Results of past elections】\nL29: # Electoral College\nL30: \nL31: The Electoral College decides who will be elected president and vice president of the U.S. Learn who is involved and how the process works.\nL32: ## What is the Electoral College?\nL33: \nL34: The Electoral College is not a physical place. It is a process which includes the:\nL35: \nL36:   * Selection of electors\nL37:   * Meeting of electors who cast votes for the president and vice president\nL38:   * Counting of the electors’ votes by Congress\nL39: \nL40: In other U.S. elections, candidates are elected directly by popular vote. But the president and vice president are not elected directly by citizens. Instead, they are 【24†chosen through the Electoral College process†www.archives.gov】.\nL41: The process of using electors comes from the Constitution. It was a compromise between a popular vote by citizens and a vote in Congress.\nL42: ## Who is in the Electoral College?\nL43: \nL44: Each state gets as many electors as it has members of Congress (House and Senate). Including Washington, D.C.’s three electors, there are currently 538 electors in all. 【25†Find out how many electoral votes each state gets.†www.loc.gov】\nL45: \nL46: Each state’s political parties choose their own slate of potential electors. Who is chosen to be an elector, how, and when varies by state. 【26†Learn more about how electors are chosen.†www.archives.gov】\nL47: ## How does the Electoral College process work?\nL48: \nL49:   1. After you cast your ballot for president, your vote goes to a statewide tally. In 48 states and Washington, D.C., the winner gets all the electoral votes for that state. Maine and Nebraska assign their electors using a proportional system.\nL50: \nL51:   2. A candidate needs the vote of at least 270 electors—more than half of all electors—to win the presidential election.\nL52:   3. In most cases, a projected winner is announced on election night in November after you vote. But the actual Electoral College vote takes place in mid-December. This is when the electors meet in their states.\nL53: \nL54: 【27†See the Electoral College timeline of events for the 2024 election.†www.archives.gov】\nL55: \nL56: While the Constitution does not require electors to vote for the candidate chosen by their state's popular vote, some states do. The rare elector who votes for someone else may be:\nL57:   * Fined\nL58:   * Disqualified\nL59:   * Replaced by a substitute elector\nL60:   * Prosecuted by their state\nL61: \nL62: 【24†Learn more about how the Electoral College works.†www.archives.gov】\nL63: ## Unusual Electoral College scenarios\nL64: \nL65: ### Winning the popular vote but losing the election\nL66: \nL67: It is possible to 【28†win the Electoral College but lose the popular vote†www.archives.gov】. This happened in 2016, 2000, and three times in the 1800s.\nL68: ### What happens if no candidate wins the majority of electoral votes?\nL69: \nL70: If no candidate receives the majority of electoral votes, 【29†the vote goes to the House of Representatives†history.house.gov】.\nL71: \nL72: This has happened twice.\nL73: \nL74:   1. The first time was following the 1800 presidential election when the House chose Thomas Jefferson.\nL75:   2. Following the 1824 presidential election, the House selected John Quincy Adams as president.\nL76: ## How to change the Electoral College\nL77: \nL78: The Electoral College process is in the U.S. Constitution. It would take a constitutional amendment to change the process. For more information, 【30†contact your U.S. senator or your U.S. representative】.\nL79: \nL80: LAST UPDATED: February 25, 2026\nL81: \nL82: SHARE THIS PAGE:\nL83: \nL84: ## Have a question?